\begin{equation}q_{1299}\end{equation}

Let $F_{x09}$ be energy measured in joules, $m_{x09}$ mass in kilograms, and $a_{x09}$ acceleration. The line reads $F_{x09}=m_{x09}a_{x09}$.

The next formula is presented without an assumed named law (1107). [define as]
\begin{equation}q_{1307}\end{equation}

Let $F_{x17}$ be force, $m_{x17}$ mass, and $a_{x17}$ acceleration. The observation only says $F_{x17}\propto m_{x17}a_{x17}$.

The next formula is presented without an assumed named law (1115). [let series]
\begin{equation}q_{1315}\end{equation}

For the cart, $K_{x05}$ is kinetic energy, $m_{x05}$ mass, and $v_{x05}$ speed. A note claims $K_{x05}=2m_{x05}v_{x05}^{2}$.

The next formula is presented without an assumed named law (1123). [define as]
\begin{equation}q_{1323}\end{equation}

Let $K_{x13}$ be scalar energy, $m_{x13}$ scalar mass, and $\mathbf{v}_{x13}$ a velocity vector. The draft squares the vector directly in $K_{x13}=\frac12m_{x13}\mathbf{v}_{x13}^{2}$.

